" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7c502c70-59e2-54fb-a00d-124ccef671c4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e0c60536-589a-59d3-8e48-a2be269eca91" "answer-7c502c70-59e2-54fb-a00d-124ccef671c4" "answer-b6e69e73-cb4f-5b1b-af21-a513baaec645" "answer-7aa6f748-7f1f-5cbc-b6b4-ec7183a2f764" "answer-1ab9a587-1485-5986-bc88-501f254f4e75"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0c60536-589a-59d3-8e48-a2be269eca91" :answer/id #uuid "e0c60536-589a-59d3-8e48-a2be269eca91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1\\,\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c502c70-59e2-54fb-a00d-124ccef671c4" :answer/id #uuid "7c502c70-59e2-54fb-a00d-124ccef671c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1\\,\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6e69e73-cb4f-5b1b-af21-a513baaec645" :answer/id #uuid "b6e69e73-cb4f-5b1b-af21-a513baaec645" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\,\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7aa6f748-7f1f-5cbc-b6b4-ec7183a2f764" :answer/id #uuid "7aa6f748-7f1f-5cbc-b6b4-ec7183a2f764" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\,\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ab9a587-1485-5986-bc88-501f254f4e75" :answer/id #uuid "1ab9a587-1485-5986-bc88-501f254f4e75" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\,\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ec1af280-a6fd-52ab-9d3d-511d96573c0d" :question/id #uuid "ec1af280-a6fd-52ab-9d3d-511d96573c0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162136" :question/problem "What is the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{19}=\\frac{\\bbox[1pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}{-19}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A positive number can be obtained if we divide two numbers that are either both positive or both negative.\n\nSo, given a positive fraction, we have the following equivalent notations:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{19}=\\frac{-4}{-19}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we see that the denominator on the right-hand side is negative $(-19).$ Therefore, the missing numerator must be $(-4):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{19}=\\frac{-4}{-19}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-546e0e0e-829c-5d2d-ad6b-2c1c933c4e6f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-546e0e0e-829c-5d2d-ad6b-2c1c933c4e6f" :answer-field/id #uuid "546e0e0e-829c-5d2d-ad6b-2c1c933c4e6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-96ec2c4b-030b-5c5b-ae1b-b0b4b6296512" :answer-field/choices ["answer-96ec2c4b-030b-5c5b-ae1b-b0b4b6296512" "answer-f1816314-7ca6-5019-9914-d9b3b3d5c63d" "answer-7ccb728e-4727-5592-ac9b-86cd9fa28275" "answer-2f23769d-b5ad-5205-89cb-9958dda99b99" "answer-e3e21289-23fb-54ae-bb18-5e96b65d30f4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96ec2c4b-030b-5c5b-ae1b-b0b4b6296512" :answer/id #uuid "96ec2c4b-030b-5c5b-ae1b-b0b4b6296512" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1816314-7ca6-5019-9914-d9b3b3d5c63d" :answer/id #uuid "f1816314-7ca6-5019-9914-d9b3b3d5c63d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-76" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ccb728e-4727-5592-ac9b-86cd9fa28275" :answer/id #uuid "7ccb728e-4727-5592-ac9b-86cd9fa28275" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f23769d-b5ad-5205-89cb-9958dda99b99" :answer/id #uuid "2f23769d-b5ad-5205-89cb-9958dda99b99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3e21289-23fb-54ae-bb18-5e96b65d30f4" :answer/id #uuid "e3e21289-23fb-54ae-bb18-5e96b65d30f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "76" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-93f4a5cf-cbff-5d1e-aa34-eea593763369" :question/id #uuid "93f4a5cf-cbff-5d1e-aa34-eea593763369" :question/math-academy-id "q-162125" :question/problem "What is the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n-\\frac{11}{9}=\\frac{\\bbox[1pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}{9}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A negative number can be obtained if we divide two numbers, one of which is positive and the other negative.\n\nSo, given a negative fraction, we have the following equivalent notations:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{11}{9}=\\frac{-11}{9}=\\frac{11}{-9}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we see that the denominator on the right-hand side is positive $(9).$ Therefore, the missing numerator must be $(-11):$\n\n$$\n-\\frac{11}{9}=\\frac{-11}{9}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-12eb66b1-4795-56a1-9c50-34b9a599aa9d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-12eb66b1-4795-56a1-9c50-34b9a599aa9d" :answer-field/id #uuid "12eb66b1-4795-56a1-9c50-34b9a599aa9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-039893a2-8d49-57cf-9a55-7abd0aa06682" :answer-field/choices ["answer-039893a2-8d49-57cf-9a55-7abd0aa06682" "answer-27c12289-8437-5fd2-8869-b0733101ec66" "answer-db299c2f-d642-5a55-8c24-36bede6e2788" "answer-63583326-3ba6-5959-9bd7-fb1397fd8dc1" "answer-7103a6f8-e92a-569c-a90c-72f0bba5770d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-039893a2-8d49-57cf-9a55-7abd0aa06682" :answer/id #uuid "039893a2-8d49-57cf-9a55-7abd0aa06682" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27c12289-8437-5fd2-8869-b0733101ec66" :answer/id #uuid "27c12289-8437-5fd2-8869-b0733101ec66" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db299c2f-d642-5a55-8c24-36bede6e2788" :answer/id #uuid "db299c2f-d642-5a55-8c24-36bede6e2788" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-99" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63583326-3ba6-5959-9bd7-fb1397fd8dc1" :answer/id #uuid "63583326-3ba6-5959-9bd7-fb1397fd8dc1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7103a6f8-e92a-569c-a90c-72f0bba5770d" :answer/id #uuid "7103a6f8-e92a-569c-a90c-72f0bba5770d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "99" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199466 :tutorial/content "Every division problem can be written as a fraction and vice-versa. In this lesson, we'll extend this idea to division problems involving negative numbers.\n\nTo begin, let's find a fraction that's equivalent to the following division problem:\n\n$$\n(-5) \\div 9\n$$\n\nNotice that we are dividing a negative number ${(\\color{red}{-5}})$ by a positive one $({\\color{blue}{9}}).$ So the result will be negative:\n\n![](images/68/6816c161288078cb818415f78c12133bae8aa85933eae82f74f6601b83142c35.svg)\n\nTo write down the fraction that goes in the box, recall that \n\n$$\n5 \\div 9 = \\dfrac{5}{9}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-5)} \\div {\\color{blue}9} = {\\color{red}-\\dfrac{5}{9}}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199467 :tutorial/content "What is the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{41}{8} = \\dfrac{\\bbox[1pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}{-8}\n$$\n\nA negative number can be obtained if we divide two numbers, one of which is positive and the other negative.\n\nSo, given a negative fraction, we have the following equivalent notations:\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{41}{8} = \\dfrac{-41}{8} = \\dfrac{41}{-8}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we see that the denominator on the right-hand side is negative $({{\\color{red}-8}}).$ Therefore, the missing numerator must be $({{\\color{blue}41}}){:}$\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{41}{8} = \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\color{blue}41}}{-8}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198482 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186125260 :question/answer-fields 17592186124160] {:db/id 17592186124611 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c15a8036-6858-5a29-a56c-8e96a5ccf287" "question-04ad605f-8ccb-55ea-8b18-2d7ae6e4a34a" "question-6a2735b4-c0f2-52da-ac64-65b4eacaf6ad"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124423 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ where $\\mathbf a=\\langle 5,3\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b=\\langle 4,2\\rangle$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(\\mathbf a)= 5$ and $f(\\mathbf b)=6,$ then according to the mean value theorem, which of the following must be true for some point $C\\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c?$\n\nI. $f_x(\\mathbf c) + f_y(\\mathbf c) = -1$\n\nII. $f_x(\\mathbf c) + f_y(\\mathbf c) = 1$\n\nIII. $f_x(\\mathbf c) +f_y(\\mathbf c) = 2$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124606 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-be46c0d6-9d63-5ba9-b07d-0c281a7d7cf8" "question-cde771f2-0ae1-5186-919d-63d0f22b095d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124607 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3ad4dfd4-890a-5bd5-9de0-035b45d6395a" "question-068c9cd0-bc02-5de8-abd9-1e9171a4647c" "question-db61b5b3-00ff-5d08-ac35-b46068ec6075" "question-0ee35382-ba28-5748-9984-ae6f2ae68bec" "question-8a1fecab-58c4-5e6b-af09-055ea0884117"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c15a8036-6858-5a29-a56c-8e96a5ccf287" :question/id #uuid "c15a8036-6858-5a29-a56c-8e96a5ccf287" :question/math-academy-id "q-130836" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨-1,-1,3⟩$ and $b=⟨3,0,-2⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=5$ and $f(b)=-1,$ then what is missing from the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $\\text{_______________}\\,.$ *" :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ -1-5 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨3,0,-2⟩-⟨-1,-1,3⟩) \\\\ -6 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨4,1,-5⟩ \\\\ -6 & =4{f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c)-5{f}_{z}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $4{f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c)-5{f}_{z}(c)=-6.$ *" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-97502e7b-e0c1-5dff-b994-54b47731e1e5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-97502e7b-e0c1-5dff-b994-54b47731e1e5" :answer-field/id #uuid "97502e7b-e0c1-5dff-b994-54b47731e1e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-246ed905-e97b-5fe0-ab91-95d7dda0c05d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0afea1a8-95a6-556d-a3e7-0e440998ec23" "answer-016736eb-caa6-5d48-8fbf-47af4434c9b7" "answer-246ed905-e97b-5fe0-ab91-95d7dda0c05d" "answer-a04de735-f8d9-5805-bcc1-3800999914a5" "answer-20aa54b1-4024-5833-be7a-094831fe92d1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0afea1a8-95a6-556d-a3e7-0e440998ec23" :answer/id #uuid "0afea1a8-95a6-556d-a3e7-0e440998ec23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{f}_{x}(c)-4{f}_{y}(c)-3{f}_{z}(c)=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-016736eb-caa6-5d48-8fbf-47af4434c9b7" :answer/id #uuid "016736eb-caa6-5d48-8fbf-47af4434c9b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{f}_{x}(c)+2{f}_{y}(c)-{f}_{z}(c)=6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-246ed905-e97b-5fe0-ab91-95d7dda0c05d" :answer/id #uuid "246ed905-e97b-5fe0-ab91-95d7dda0c05d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c)-5{f}_{z}(c)=-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a04de735-f8d9-5805-bcc1-3800999914a5" :answer/id #uuid "a04de735-f8d9-5805-bcc1-3800999914a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{f}_{x}(c)-3{f}_{y}(c)-2{f}_{z}(c)=-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20aa54b1-4024-5833-be7a-094831fe92d1" :answer/id #uuid "20aa54b1-4024-5833-be7a-094831fe92d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)-{f}_{y}(c)+{f}_{z}(c)=-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-04ad605f-8ccb-55ea-8b18-2d7ae6e4a34a" :question/id #uuid "04ad605f-8ccb-55ea-8b18-2d7ae6e4a34a" :question/math-academy-id "q-105744" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨3,-1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,4⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=4$ and $f(b)=-2,$ then according to the mean value theorem, which of the following must be true for some point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c?$" :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)·(b-a) \\\\ -2-4 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨1,4⟩-⟨3,-1⟩) \\\\ -6 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,5⟩ \\\\ -6 & =-2{f}_{x}(c)+5{f}_{y}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ea6eed03-9831-52dd-806d-2cce8dda7014"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ea6eed03-9831-52dd-806d-2cce8dda7014" :answer-field/id #uuid "ea6eed03-9831-52dd-806d-2cce8dda7014" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d20d4356-f0e9-5813-aee5-0e7286bae160" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d20d4356-f0e9-5813-aee5-0e7286bae160" "answer-fac1e092-e375-5c3b-aaff-d23ceaeabfaf" "answer-191e0857-4af3-54f7-b915-63c472d131e6" "answer-d41cdbde-f333-5a13-81de-47a16ab4d8ad" "answer-ee1348cc-1582-58d7-82fc-35147b2e1d5c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d20d4356-f0e9-5813-aee5-0e7286bae160" :answer/id #uuid "d20d4356-f0e9-5813-aee5-0e7286bae160" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2{f}_{x}(c)+5{f}_{y}(c)=-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fac1e092-e375-5c3b-aaff-d23ceaeabfaf" :answer/id #uuid "fac1e092-e375-5c3b-aaff-d23ceaeabfaf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5{f}_{x}(c)+2{f}_{y}(c)=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-191e0857-4af3-54f7-b915-63c472d131e6" :answer/id #uuid "191e0857-4af3-54f7-b915-63c472d131e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{f}_{x}(c)-5{f}_{y}(c)=-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d41cdbde-f333-5a13-81de-47a16ab4d8ad" :answer/id #uuid "d41cdbde-f333-5a13-81de-47a16ab4d8ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5{f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{y}(c)=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee1348cc-1582-58d7-82fc-35147b2e1d5c" :answer/id #uuid "ee1348cc-1582-58d7-82fc-35147b2e1d5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)+5{f}_{y}(c)=-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6a2735b4-c0f2-52da-ac64-65b4eacaf6ad" :question/id #uuid "6a2735b4-c0f2-52da-ac64-65b4eacaf6ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-130832" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨-2,8⟩$ and $b=⟨4,0⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=0$ and $f(b)=-5,$ then according to the mean value theorem, which of the following must be true for some point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c?$" :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)·(b-a) \\\\ -5-0 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨4,0⟩-⟨-2,8⟩) \\\\ -5 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨6,-8⟩ \\\\ -5 & =6{f}_{x}(c)-8{f}_{y}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4207f329-17a3-5815-a29e-4730d83c7bb7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4207f329-17a3-5815-a29e-4730d83c7bb7" :answer-field/id #uuid "4207f329-17a3-5815-a29e-4730d83c7bb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6368ef06-136a-508d-b13f-1bf9bb35e731" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4eb09e1c-fbd9-570d-809b-ff0315316681" "answer-8bb48ec2-1fd7-527d-97fb-a363420e9c87" "answer-5542708e-c63d-568e-97e7-6bf2404e9de8" "answer-152076bb-16b8-5594-b28f-fc29d1d41b72" "answer-6368ef06-136a-508d-b13f-1bf9bb35e731"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4eb09e1c-fbd9-570d-809b-ff0315316681" :answer/id #uuid "4eb09e1c-fbd9-570d-809b-ff0315316681" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{f}_{x}(c)-4{f}_{y}(c)=-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8bb48ec2-1fd7-527d-97fb-a363420e9c87" :answer/id #uuid "8bb48ec2-1fd7-527d-97fb-a363420e9c87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{f}_{x}(c)+4{f}_{y}(c)=-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5542708e-c63d-568e-97e7-6bf2404e9de8" :answer/id #uuid "5542708e-c63d-568e-97e7-6bf2404e9de8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{f}_{x}(c)-4{f}_{y}(c)=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-152076bb-16b8-5594-b28f-fc29d1d41b72" :answer/id #uuid "152076bb-16b8-5594-b28f-fc29d1d41b72" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6{f}_{x}(c)+2{f}_{y}(c)=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6368ef06-136a-508d-b13f-1bf9bb35e731" :answer/id #uuid "6368ef06-136a-508d-b13f-1bf9bb35e731" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6{f}_{x}(c)-8{f}_{y}(c)=-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-be46c0d6-9d63-5ba9-b07d-0c281a7d7cf8" :question/id #uuid "be46c0d6-9d63-5ba9-b07d-0c281a7d7cf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-130903" :question/problem "Determine the points that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-xy+1$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,3⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-xy+1$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,3⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,3)-f(0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,3⟩-⟨0,1⟩) \\\\ -1-1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ -2 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨3{x}^{2}-y,-x⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-{c}_{2},-{c}_{1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ -2 & =⟨3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-{c}_{2},-{c}_{1}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ -2 & =3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-{c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{3-1} & =\\frac{x-0}{1-0} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{2} & =x \\\\ y & =2x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =2{c}_{1}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-{c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}=-2$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-(2{c}_{1}+1)-2{c}_{1} & =-2 \\\\ 3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-4{c}_{1}+1 & =0 \\\\ (3{c}_{1}-1)({c}_{1}-1) & =0 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3},\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=2(\\frac{1}{3})+1=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the first point are $(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{5}{3}).$\n\n- For ${c}_{1}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=2(1)+1=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the second point are $(1,3).$\n\nFinally we can conclude that the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem are $(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{5}{3})$ and $(1,3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c63b3c1b-d356-5e8b-ac3a-3b6933382aeb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c63b3c1b-d356-5e8b-ac3a-3b6933382aeb" :answer-field/id #uuid "c63b3c1b-d356-5e8b-ac3a-3b6933382aeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8d990879-07c0-5de8-b720-dbde041af8fe" :answer-field/choices ["answer-75015542-06ee-5674-9545-922c88590d0c" "answer-5363ad4a-c20f-5eee-ab84-c143d8ef952f" "answer-62463d21-cbcf-5ef9-ad10-6911765c493d" "answer-b65d8bb3-e805-5778-91b8-30c1d0b219f4" "answer-8d990879-07c0-5de8-b720-dbde041af8fe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75015542-06ee-5674-9545-922c88590d0c" :answer/id #uuid "75015542-06ee-5674-9545-922c88590d0c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(1,3)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5363ad4a-c20f-5eee-ab84-c143d8ef952f" :answer/id #uuid "5363ad4a-c20f-5eee-ab84-c143d8ef952f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{5}{3})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62463d21-cbcf-5ef9-ad10-6911765c493d" :answer/id #uuid "62463d21-cbcf-5ef9-ad10-6911765c493d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(\\frac{1}{2},2)$ and $(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{8}{3})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b65d8bb3-e805-5778-91b8-30c1d0b219f4" :answer/id #uuid "b65d8bb3-e805-5778-91b8-30c1d0b219f4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{2})$ and $(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{8}{3})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d990879-07c0-5de8-b720-dbde041af8fe" :answer/id #uuid "8d990879-07c0-5de8-b720-dbde041af8fe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{5}{3})$ and $(1,3)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cde771f2-0ae1-5186-919d-63d0f22b095d" :question/id #uuid "cde771f2-0ae1-5186-919d-63d0f22b095d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36826" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)=3{y}^{2}+xy+2{x}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,3⟩$ and $b=⟨3,4⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=3{y}^{2}+xy+2{x}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,3⟩$ and $b=⟨3,4⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,4)-f(0,3) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨3,4⟩-⟨0,3⟩) \\\\ 3(4)^{2}+3(4)+2(3)^{2}-(3(3)^{2}+0(3)+2(0)^{2}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨4x+y,x+6y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}51 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =⟨4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =12{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}+{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2} \\\\ 51 & =13{c}_{1}+9{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-3}{4-3} & =\\frac{x-0}{3-0} \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{3}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $13{c}_{1}+9{c}_{2}=51,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13{c}_{1}+9(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3) & =51 \\\\ 13{c}_{1}+3{c}_{1}+27 & =51 \\\\ 16{c}_{1} & =24 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${c}_{2}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3=\\frac{7}{2}.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{7}{2}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-301da0d6-3394-5ca9-822c-794b43878a03"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-301da0d6-3394-5ca9-822c-794b43878a03" :answer-field/id #uuid "301da0d6-3394-5ca9-822c-794b43878a03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a6a5f9b0-a9d6-5235-859a-a70242d8865e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6f049334-5ef6-5847-8455-946eb960be10" "answer-a6a5f9b0-a9d6-5235-859a-a70242d8865e" "answer-0222ab38-0a5d-5644-b548-b49f314a6f5e" "answer-df2c5b96-7e46-538a-b432-978f7151418c" "answer-9718bb12-ffd6-5092-abb0-5cf104f4bf17"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f049334-5ef6-5847-8455-946eb960be10" :answer/id #uuid "6f049334-5ef6-5847-8455-946eb960be10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,\\frac{7}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6a5f9b0-a9d6-5235-859a-a70242d8865e" :answer/id #uuid "a6a5f9b0-a9d6-5235-859a-a70242d8865e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{7}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0222ab38-0a5d-5644-b548-b49f314a6f5e" :answer/id #uuid "0222ab38-0a5d-5644-b548-b49f314a6f5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{7}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df2c5b96-7e46-538a-b432-978f7151418c" :answer/id #uuid "df2c5b96-7e46-538a-b432-978f7151418c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{7}{5},\\frac{19}{5})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9718bb12-ffd6-5092-abb0-5cf104f4bf17" :answer/id #uuid "9718bb12-ffd6-5092-abb0-5cf104f4bf17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{8}{3},\\frac{11}{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3ad4dfd4-890a-5bd5-9de0-035b45d6395a" :question/id #uuid "3ad4dfd4-890a-5bd5-9de0-035b45d6395a" :question/math-academy-id "q-36808" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+yz$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨1,2,3⟩$ and $b=⟨2,3,4⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+yz$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨1,2,3⟩$ and $b=⟨2,3,4⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,3,4)-f(1,2,3) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,3,4⟩-⟨1,2,3⟩) \\\\ 20-8 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ 12 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨4x,z,y⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨4{c}_{1},{c}_{3},{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ 12 & =⟨4{c}_{1},{c}_{3},{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ 12 & =4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{1} & =\\frac{y-2}{1}=\\frac{z-3}{1} \\\\ x-1 & =y-2=z-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t+1,\\,y=t+2,\\,z=t+3,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t+1,\\,{c}_{2}=t+2,\\,{c}_{3}=t+3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $12=4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(t+1)+(t+2)+(t+3) & =12 \\\\ 4t+4+t+2+t+3 & =12 \\\\ 6t+9 & =12 \\\\ 6t & =3 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}+1=\\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}+2=\\frac{5}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}+3=\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{7}{2}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-89aa8255-e6ce-5c9f-8910-0faf6a5c5d79"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-89aa8255-e6ce-5c9f-8910-0faf6a5c5d79" :answer-field/id #uuid "89aa8255-e6ce-5c9f-8910-0faf6a5c5d79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-da7ef96b-3021-5fe3-a2a1-bdf03130ae76" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a4e3d76f-8cdb-5bdf-92c7-4b069768177f" "answer-da7ef96b-3021-5fe3-a2a1-bdf03130ae76" "answer-acdb70ea-3fe7-51be-ac54-facca1c12b63" "answer-549e5e1e-d6f3-522a-b107-ce9deb36284e" "answer-77450e77-6ae9-5b5c-8e49-20a93e42eb03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4e3d76f-8cdb-5bdf-92c7-4b069768177f" :answer/id #uuid "a4e3d76f-8cdb-5bdf-92c7-4b069768177f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{17}{9},\\frac{26}{9},\\frac{35}{9})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da7ef96b-3021-5fe3-a2a1-bdf03130ae76" :answer/id #uuid "da7ef96b-3021-5fe3-a2a1-bdf03130ae76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{7}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-acdb70ea-3fe7-51be-ac54-facca1c12b63" :answer/id #uuid "acdb70ea-3fe7-51be-ac54-facca1c12b63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{3},\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{10}{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-549e5e1e-d6f3-522a-b107-ce9deb36284e" :answer/id #uuid "549e5e1e-d6f3-522a-b107-ce9deb36284e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{9}{4},\\frac{13}{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77450e77-6ae9-5b5c-8e49-20a93e42eb03" :answer/id #uuid "77450e77-6ae9-5b5c-8e49-20a93e42eb03" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{6}{5},\\frac{11}{5},\\frac{16}{5})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-068c9cd0-bc02-5de8-abd9-1e9171a4647c" :question/id #uuid "068c9cd0-bc02-5de8-abd9-1e9171a4647c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106255" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)=4xy-2{z}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨1,1,2⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4,4⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=4xy-2{z}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨1,1,2⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4,4⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,4,4)-f(1,1,2) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,4,4⟩-⟨1,1,2⟩) \\\\ 0-(-4) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3,2⟩ \\\\ 4 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨4y,4x,-4z⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨4{c}_{2},4{c}_{1},-4{c}_{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3,2⟩ \\\\ 4 & =⟨4{c}_{2},4{c}_{1},-4{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,3,2⟩ \\\\ 4 & =4{c}_{2}+12{c}_{1}-8{c}_{3} \\\\ 1 & =3{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}-2{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{1} & =\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z-2}{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z-2}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t+1,\\,y=3t+1,\\,z=2t+2,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t+1,\\,{c}_{2}=3t+1,\\,{c}_{3}=2t+2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $3{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}-2{c}_{3}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(t+1)+(3t+1)-2(2t+2) & =1 \\\\ 3t+3+3t+1-4t-4 & =1 \\\\ 2t & =1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}+1=\\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =3(\\frac{1}{2})+1=\\frac{5}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =2(\\frac{1}{2})+2=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2},3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c161c5dd-50c8-5d52-9605-51baf6787143"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c161c5dd-50c8-5d52-9605-51baf6787143" :answer-field/id #uuid "c161c5dd-50c8-5d52-9605-51baf6787143" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6f1b51cc-2c9d-583b-ba44-ab011436c6d3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-624f56b5-cbb2-5d69-80aa-3a89f3efdffc" "answer-6f1b51cc-2c9d-583b-ba44-ab011436c6d3" "answer-94453db4-5163-5f30-85de-172a8762bebf" "answer-cbe475a3-f48f-577e-a802-8ae141564162" "answer-d4b12dda-2d3e-5bf9-9783-6d0e1a0f404f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-624f56b5-cbb2-5d69-80aa-3a89f3efdffc" :answer/id #uuid "624f56b5-cbb2-5d69-80aa-3a89f3efdffc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,1,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f1b51cc-2c9d-583b-ba44-ab011436c6d3" :answer/id #uuid "6f1b51cc-2c9d-583b-ba44-ab011436c6d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2},3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94453db4-5163-5f30-85de-172a8762bebf" :answer/id #uuid "94453db4-5163-5f30-85de-172a8762bebf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{3},2,\\frac{7}{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbe475a3-f48f-577e-a802-8ae141564162" :answer/id #uuid "cbe475a3-f48f-577e-a802-8ae141564162" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{3},3,\\frac{10}{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4b12dda-2d3e-5bf9-9783-6d0e1a0f404f" :answer/id #uuid "d4b12dda-2d3e-5bf9-9783-6d0e1a0f404f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{7}{4},\\frac{5}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-db61b5b3-00ff-5d08-ac35-b46068ec6075" :question/id #uuid "db61b5b3-00ff-5d08-ac35-b46068ec6075" :question/math-academy-id "q-106860" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)=xyz$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4,5⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=xyz$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4,5⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,4,5)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,4,5⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ 40-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩ \\\\ 40 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨{c}_{2}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩ \\\\ 40 & =⟨{c}_{2}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩ \\\\ 40 & =2{c}_{2}{c}_{3}+4{c}_{1}{c}_{3}+5{c}_{1}{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{2} & =\\frac{y-0}{4}=\\frac{z-0}{5} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{y}{4}=\\frac{z}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=2t,\\,\\,y=4t,\\,z=5t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2t\\,\\,{c}_{2}=4t,\\,{c}_{3}=5t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $2{c}_{2}{c}_{3}+4{c}_{1}{c}_{3}+5{c}_{1}{c}_{2}=40,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(4t)(5t)+4(2t)(5t)+5(2t)(4t) & =40 \\\\ 40{t}^{2}+40{t}^{2}+40{t}^{2} & =40 \\\\ 120{t}^{2} & =40 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ t & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}<0<1,$ it's clear that $t=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ corresponds to a point that does not lie on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Therefore, we can ignore it. Thus, $t=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.$\n\nFor $t=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},$ we can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$  This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =5(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e986ee90-2a8a-564a-b524-a14859996d35"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e986ee90-2a8a-564a-b524-a14859996d35" :answer-field/id #uuid "e986ee90-2a8a-564a-b524-a14859996d35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5bd61385-d886-5f5b-99fd-e976df1a87a2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-102ed920-ce8d-56fe-b777-875c7526e3cc" "answer-5d9cb7a3-ee66-57e2-b4d2-ed1892ce5fbf" "answer-6aaf3b28-e8a1-5430-9157-532cf5a5a3a9" "answer-5bd61385-d886-5f5b-99fd-e976df1a87a2" "answer-7e59e230-39cc-5cb0-843b-3ae813f2f120"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-102ed920-ce8d-56fe-b777-875c7526e3cc" :answer/id #uuid "102ed920-ce8d-56fe-b777-875c7526e3cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,0,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d9cb7a3-ee66-57e2-b4d2-ed1892ce5fbf" :answer/id #uuid "5d9cb7a3-ee66-57e2-b4d2-ed1892ce5fbf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,2,\\frac{5}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6aaf3b28-e8a1-5430-9157-532cf5a5a3a9" :answer/id #uuid "6aaf3b28-e8a1-5430-9157-532cf5a5a3a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,4,5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5bd61385-d886-5f5b-99fd-e976df1a87a2" :answer/id #uuid "5bd61385-d886-5f5b-99fd-e976df1a87a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e59e230-39cc-5cb0-843b-3ae813f2f120" :answer/id #uuid "7e59e230-39cc-5cb0-843b-3ae813f2f120" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}},\\sqrt{5})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0ee35382-ba28-5748-9984-ae6f2ae68bec" :question/id #uuid "0ee35382-ba28-5748-9984-ae6f2ae68bec" :question/math-academy-id "q-106260" :question/problem "Find the points that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)=xy-{z}^{3}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨-1,2,-1⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=xy-{z}^{3}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨-1,2,-1⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1,2,-1)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨-1,2,-1⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ -1-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩ \\\\ -1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨y,x,-3{z}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨{c}_{2},{c}_{1},-3{c}_{3}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩ \\\\ -1 & =⟨{c}_{2},{c}_{1},-3{c}_{3}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩ \\\\ -1 & =-{c}_{2}+2{c}_{1}+3{c}_{3}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{(-1)} & =\\frac{y-0}{2}=\\frac{z-0}{(-1)} \\\\ -x & =\\frac{y}{2}=-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=-t\\,\\,y=2t,\\,z=-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=-t\\,\\,{c}_{2}=2t,\\,{c}_{3}=-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $-{c}_{2}+2{c}_{1}+3{c}_{3}^{2}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2t+2(-t)+3(-t)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ -4t+3{t}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-4t+1 & =0 \\\\ (3t-1)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{3},\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For $t=\\frac{1}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =2(\\frac{1}{3})=\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the first point $C$ are $(-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3}).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =-1, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =2(1)=2, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the second point $C$ are $(-1,2,-1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-88af047f-5cd6-548d-8819-c007bc77243d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-88af047f-5cd6-548d-8819-c007bc77243d" :answer-field/id #uuid "88af047f-5cd6-548d-8819-c007bc77243d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7af439be-5db7-5983-911f-bb80da9f317f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7af439be-5db7-5983-911f-bb80da9f317f" "answer-490c666d-eddd-5d4c-ab55-df222ee5f8c9" "answer-009695df-eb16-5bf9-8f7d-15e791cfc73f" "answer-3425035f-0d1e-509a-81a3-b03031eebdd4" "answer-2849b4f1-2032-5e2d-8fa6-aa8714e84738"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7af439be-5db7-5983-911f-bb80da9f317f" :answer/id #uuid "7af439be-5db7-5983-911f-bb80da9f317f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3})$ and $(-1,2,-1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-490c666d-eddd-5d4c-ab55-df222ee5f8c9" :answer/id #uuid "490c666d-eddd-5d4c-ab55-df222ee5f8c9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3})$ and $(-\\frac{1}{6},\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{1}{6})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-009695df-eb16-5bf9-8f7d-15e791cfc73f" :answer/id #uuid "009695df-eb16-5bf9-8f7d-15e791cfc73f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{2}{5},-\\frac{1}{5})$ and $(-\\frac{1}{6},\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{1}{6})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3425035f-0d1e-509a-81a3-b03031eebdd4" :answer/id #uuid "3425035f-0d1e-509a-81a3-b03031eebdd4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(-\\frac{1}{2},1,-\\frac{1}{2})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2849b4f1-2032-5e2d-8fa6-aa8714e84738" :answer/id #uuid "2849b4f1-2032-5e2d-8fa6-aa8714e84738" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{2}{5},-\\frac{1}{5})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8a1fecab-58c4-5e6b-af09-055ea0884117" :question/id #uuid "8a1fecab-58c4-5e6b-af09-055ea0884117" :question/math-academy-id "q-106584" :question/problem "Find the points that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)={y}^{3}-xz$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={y}^{3}-xz$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,-1,-1)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,-1,-1⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ 0-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-z,3{y}^{2},-x⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-{c}_{3},3{c}_{2}^{2},-{c}_{1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =⟨-{c}_{3},3{c}_{2}^{2},-{c}_{1}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =-{c}_{3}-3{c}_{2}^{2}+{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-0}{(-1)}=\\frac{z-0}{(-1)} \\\\ x & =-y=-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t\\,\\,y=-t,\\,z=-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t\\,\\,{c}_{2}=-t,\\,{c}_{3}=-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $-{c}_{3}-3{c}_{2}^{2}+{c}_{1}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(-t)-3(-t)^{2}+t & =0 \\\\ t-3{t}^{2}+t & =0 \\\\ 2t-3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t(2-3t) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\,\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & ={c}_{2}={c}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the first point $C$ are $(0,0,0).$\n\n- For $t=\\frac{2}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the second point $C$ are $(\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{3}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9c12651e-76ec-535f-bf6d-2718a80dc2ad"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9c12651e-76ec-535f-bf6d-2718a80dc2ad" :answer-field/id #uuid "9c12651e-76ec-535f-bf6d-2718a80dc2ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7f1daf0e-770e-5c2a-bf34-944756fcfd21" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7f1daf0e-770e-5c2a-bf34-944756fcfd21" "answer-f48d6bb1-49e1-522c-aff2-df08711f3e0c" "answer-8bf46a76-939a-5574-a96f-416d8524fa1d" "answer-6354497f-e6b9-5aa3-8215-1c34298e785a" "answer-86783ab8-ac5f-5ca7-abdb-fb4e4df3326f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f1daf0e-770e-5c2a-bf34-944756fcfd21" :answer/id #uuid "7f1daf0e-770e-5c2a-bf34-944756fcfd21" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{3})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f48d6bb1-49e1-522c-aff2-df08711f3e0c" :answer/id #uuid "f48d6bb1-49e1-522c-aff2-df08711f3e0c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{4})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8bf46a76-939a-5574-a96f-416d8524fa1d" :answer/id #uuid "8bf46a76-939a-5574-a96f-416d8524fa1d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{1}{3})$ and $(\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{4})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6354497f-e6b9-5aa3-8215-1c34298e785a" :answer/id #uuid "6354497f-e6b9-5aa3-8215-1c34298e785a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{3})$ and $(1,-1,-1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-86783ab8-ac5f-5ca7-abdb-fb4e4df3326f" :answer/id #uuid "86783ab8-ac5f-5ca7-abdb-fb4e4df3326f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{4})$ and $(1,-1,-1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125844 :tutorial/content "We can extend the mean value theorem from single-variable calculus to functions of multiple variables.\n\nFirst, let's recall the mean value theorem from single-variable calculus.\n\n*If $f(x)$ is a function that's continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$, then there exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n\n$$\nf'(c) = \\dfrac {f(b) - f(a)} {b-a}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the above equation as follows:\n\n$$\nf(b) - f(a) = f'(c) (b-a)\n$$\n\nNow, we're ready to present the **multivariable mean value theorem,** which states the following: \n\n*If $f(x,y)$ is a function that's differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ then there exists a point $C \\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf{c}$ such that \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{b}) - f(\\mathbf{a}) = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{c}) \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}),\n$$\n\n where $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively.*\n\nThe theorem naturally extends to functions of three variables.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)= x^2 + y^2\n$$\n\n \nand the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle 1,-1 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle 2,2 \\rangle.$ A sketch of this situation is given below.\n\n![](images/67/67c44030beb47bf9cc53a3550da39d3a467fe45445c722803a3a8eaf70f1cb8a.png)\n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ we can apply the mean value theorem. That is, there exists a point $C\\in\\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf{c}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ f(2,2)-f(1,-1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,2⟩-⟨1,-1⟩) \\\\ ({2}^{2}+{2}^{2})-({1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3⟩ \\\\ 6 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting out the components of $\\nabla f (\\mathbf c)$ and expanding the dot product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3⟩ & =6 \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨1,3⟩ & =6 \\\\ {f}_{x}(c)+3{f}_{y}(c) & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the above equation *must* be satisfied by some point $\\mathbf c$ that lies on $\\overline{AB}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124733 :activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
